<h2>The case in one view</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Case</th><td>Smt. Harvinder Kaur v. Smt. Saranpreet Kaur &amp; Sh. Amandeep Singh Johar</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Court</th><td>Ms. Helly Fur Kaur, Civil Judge-04 (West), Tis Hazari Courts, Delhi</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Pronounced</th><td>3 November 2026 (suit instituted in 2015)</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Provisions</th><td>Sections 38 and 41(h), Specific Relief Act; Sections 2(s), 17 and 19, DV Act</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Suit decreed; permanent injunction against both defendants</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The plaintiff, a senior citizen, owned a flat in Janakpuri under a registered conveyance deed of 2009. Her case was that her son and daughter-in-law, after moving out, repeatedly returned to the flat, quarrelled, demanded money and a share in the house, and threatened her; she pointed to incidents of June and July 2015, a payment of Rs 45,000 which she said was extracted under pressure, and multiple police complaints. She sued in 2015 for a permanent injunction restraining the defendants from visiting the flat.</p>
<p>The son pleaded that his father had helped purchase the flat; the daughter-in-law called it joint property. Neither led evidence. The daughter-in-law was eventually proceeded ex parte in December 2024, and the suit went to judgment a decade after institution.</p>

<h2>Better title, and what it carried</h2>
<p>On the documents, the court accepted the plaintiff&rsquo;s ownership. In the words of the judgment, the plaintiff &ldquo;can be said to be the owner of the suit property or at least have better title over the defendants.&rdquo; The defendants&rsquo; competing versions remained bare pleadings without proof. The spousal-contribution argument also ran into the statutory framework governing benami claims, which the court considered alongside the evidence.</p>

<h2>Section 17(2): exclusion through due process</h2>
<p>The legally significant step is the court&rsquo;s treatment of the DV Act. It accepted that the flat could be a &ldquo;shared household&rdquo; under Section 2(s), carrying a right of residence for the daughter-in-law under Section 17(1). But Section 17(2) provides that the aggrieved person shall not be evicted or excluded from the shared household <em>except in accordance with the procedure established by law</em>. Following the Supreme Court in <em>Satish Chander Ahuja v. Sneha Ahuja</em> (2020), the court held that a civil suit before a competent court is precisely such a procedure. The suit was therefore maintainable, and on the evidence, it was decreed.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>The working rule after Ahuja:</strong> the shared-household right protects against extra-legal ouster — lockouts, forced removals, strong-arm tactics. It does not immunise the occupant against a civil decree passed after trial, where her defences, and any claim to alternate accommodation in DV proceedings, can be duly weighed.</p>
</div>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For senior-citizen owners.</strong> The civil suit remains a full remedy against relatives asserting possession of the owner&rsquo;s property. Registered title, coherent pleadings and proof of the incidents relied upon carried the day here.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For daughters-in-law.</strong> The residence right under Section 17 is real, but it must be defended: contest the suit, lead evidence, and where appropriate seek a residence order or alternate accommodation under Section 19 of the DV Act in parallel proceedings. An ex parte course surrenders the contest.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For pleaders on both sides.</strong> Claims of contribution to the purchase, or of joint ownership, fail without evidence. A bare assertion in the written statement proves nothing after trial.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s courts see this triangle constantly: an owner (often a parent), a son, and a daughter-in-law asserting shared-household rights. <em>Satish Chander Ahuja</em> reset the balance in 2020 — the definition of shared household is wide, but the owner&rsquo;s civil remedies survive, and the two regimes are reconciled through Section 17(2)&rsquo;s due-process clause. This Tis Hazari judgment is a straightforward application at the trial-court level: title proved, incidents established, defences unproved, decree follows. The careful practitioner will also note what the decree does not decide — the daughter-in-law&rsquo;s DV Act reliefs, which belong to the Magistrate&rsquo;s jurisdiction and remain governed by the Act&rsquo;s own scheme.</p>
